Chapter: ProbeClosureLuck

 Tests whether the correct answer is luck. The chapter is
 probe-closure-solo's, which printed 47. The driver evaluates the SAME
 expression twice, with one unrelated call in between to disturb whatever
 the registers happen to hold.

 If a and c both read 47, the result is stable and the 47 is real.
 If a reads 47 and c does not, then the value of an expression depends on
 what ran before it, and every correct answer in this whole series is
 provisional -- including the ones used as controls.

Section: Shapes

  add3 : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  add3 (x) (y) (z) = x + y + z

  make-adder : Integer -> (Integer, Integer -> Integer)
  make-adder (n) = add3 n

Section: Driver

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni ("a 47 expected: " & show (((make-adder 5) 20) 22))
    print-line-uni ("b 6 expected: " & show (((add3 1) 2) 3))
    print-line-uni ("c 47 expected: " & show (((make-adder 5) 20) 22))
  end
